      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "780557a1-c2aa-5c68-a365-10935890820c",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Doggerland, southern North Sea, Great Britain, continental Europe, North Atlantic Ocean\n- Events: last glacial period, Mesolithic period, Storegga Slide, tsunami\n- Concepts: sea-level rise, submergence, landslides, human habitation\n\nFacts:\n- Doggerland was an area in what is now the southern North Sea that connected Great Britain to continental Europe during and after the last glacial period.\n- Doggerland was probably a rich habitat with human habitation during the Mesolithic period.\n- Rising sea levels gradually reduced Doggerland to low-lying islands before its final submergence.\n- The three Storegga Slides are considered among the largest known landslides.\n- The Storegga Slide collapse involved an estimated 290 km length of coastal shelf and about 3500 km3 of debris.\n- The Storegga Slide collapse caused a very large tsunami in the North Atlantic Ocean, which may have contributed to the final submergence of Doggerland.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "07e29a2f-06f7-597f-ad0e-02bd957ba27b",
        "text": "Page: Doggerland. Sentence: Doggerland was an area now beneath the southern North Sea that connected Great Britain to continental Europe during and after the last glacial period.\nPage: Doggerland. Sentence: It was probably a rich habitat with human habitation in the Mesolithic period, although rising sea levels gradually reduced it to low-lying islands before its final submergence, possibly following a tsunami caused by the Storegga Slide.\nPage: Storegga Slide. Sentence: The three Storegga Slides are considered to be amongst the largest known landslides.\nPage: Storegga Slide. Sentence: The collapse involved an estimated 290 km length of coastal shelf, with a total volume of 3500 km3 of debris, which caused a very large tsunami in the North Atlantic Ocean.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
